<h2>The medical record that mattered</h2>
<p>The application under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 did not rest on a diagnosis alone. It rested on a chain of verifiable facts: a past surgery for testicular seminoma; the oncological reality that seminoma demands surveillance for recurrence; a reported weight loss of about five kilograms over four months; a last scan dated 2025; and — decisively — the failure of the custodial medical system to close that gap. The Court had earlier directed an examination, but the Rajiv Gandhi Super Speciality Hospital, to which the jail refers inmates, has no oncology department. The Medical Officer of Central Jail No. 11, Mandoli, recommended referral to the Delhi State Cancer Institute.</p>

<h2>A narrowly tailored release</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Purpose-bound.</strong> The release is limited to examination and tests at the Delhi State Cancer Institute, East Dilshad Garden — not liberty at large.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Time-bound.</strong> Three weeks from release, with surrender on expiry built into the order.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Accountable.</strong> The test results must be submitted; the applicant must stay within NCT Delhi, keep his phone on, notify address changes, and keep clear of the complainant and witnesses.</div>
</div>
<p>The bond was fixed at Rs 50,000 with one surety, and the order preserved the State&rsquo;s remedy: any violation can be raised by the Additional Public Prosecutor and would lead to cancellation.</p>

<h2>How medical interim bail is actually won</h2>
<p>Medical grounds are among the most commonly pleaded and most commonly rejected bases for interim release, because courts have learned to distinguish illness from unmanageable illness. The jurisdiction responds not to the seriousness of a condition in the abstract but to the mismatch between what the condition requires and what custodial healthcare can deliver. Three features of this application repay study. First, the condition was documented through the jail&rsquo;s own medical officer — the recommendation for referral came from inside the custodial system, not merely from the applicant&rsquo;s side. Second, the institutional gap was specific and undeniable: a cancer follow-up cannot happen at a hospital with no oncology department. Third, the relief sought matched the need — weeks for testing, not months of open-ended liberty.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Element</th><th>Weak application</th><th>This application</th></tr>
