  <tr><td>Custodial gap</td><td>&ldquo;Jail treatment inadequate&rdquo; asserted generally</td><td>Referral hospital concretely lacks an oncology department</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Relief sought</td><td>Open-ended bail on health grounds</td><td>Three weeks, tied to a named institute, results to be filed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Route the record through the jail.</strong> Directions for a medical board or the jail MO&rsquo;s report convert a private grievance into institutional evidence.</p>
  <p><strong>Identify the facility gap.</strong> Name the hospital in the referral chain and show what it cannot do; that specificity is what separates this order from the routine refusals.</p>
  <p><strong>Draft the conditions yourself.</strong> Offering a purpose-bound, time-bound structure — where the applicant goes, for how long, filing what proof — makes granting easy.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
